Found on Windows 2026-11-22 (Windows 11 Pro 26H2 build 26300.9711, Node 22.17.1, npm 10.9.2, unreagent d4edd23) while setting up the UE 5.8 example project.

prepareNodeBridge runs a bare "npm install" in Plugin/UELLMToolkit/Resources/mcp-bridge. With the Toolkit at 9f6a0f7 it exits 1: "npm error Cannot read properties of null (reading 'edgesOut')", stack in @npmcli/arborist build-ideal-tree.js #loadPeerSet, while npm resolves the peers of the bridge's devDependencies (vitest ^4.0.18 -> vitest 4.1.12 -> @vitest/browser-* ...). Same with --omit=dev, with npm ci, and with package-lock.json removed. Works: npm install --legacy-peer-deps (with --omit=dev: 89 packages, @modelcontextprotocol/sdk 1.25.1), and npx npm@11.21.3 install.

Under the launcher (example run 41): with node_modules already there, installed by the example's setup script, the WARN says the launcher keeps them, the smoke test passes, the marker is written, and later starts skip the install. On a fresh clone without the setup script there is no node_modules, so "bridge will not start".

Second effect: upstream's package-lock.json does not match its package.json (lock: name unrealclaude-mcp-bridge 1.1.0 with zod; package.json: ue5-mcp-server 1.3.0 with vitest dev deps), so every successful install rewrites the tracked lock file and leaves a submodule checkout dirty.

Candidates (unreagent-worker): install bridges with "npm install --omit=dev --legacy-peer-deps" (a bridge needs its runtime deps only), or make the install arguments configurable; mention the lock-file rewrite in the README's Toolkit section. The example's setup script now runs the install itself with those flags and restores the lock file afterwards.

