Choice (mine): plain npm install first; one retry with --legacy-peer-deps, within the same 5-minute bridgeInstallTimeout, only when the plain install exits non-zero and npm names no error code (an exception inside npm, like this crash) or ERESOLVE. Why: fixed --legacy-peer-deps would silently drop auto-installed peers for every bridge, and --omit=dev does not avoid the crash (the ideal tree is built with dev deps either way) while it would break a bridge whose install script needs a dev dependency. Every bridge that installs today keeps exactly the same npm call. Any other npm code (network, registry, file access, install script) gets no retry, because the flag cannot change it and the network case costs 141 s per try. No config option: the retry needs none, and npm's own config (npm_config_* in the environment, a user .npmrc) already reaches the launcher's npm, which inherits the environment.

Lock file (decision for Getty, not implemented): every successful install rewrites upstream's out-of-sync package-lock.json (+1570/-65 lines), the retry as well. Measured: --no-save leaves the lock byte-identical and still installs its pins (SDK 1.25.1, 152 packages); for a bridge without a lock it writes none (a plain install would add an untracked package-lock.json), and npm still adds peers on its own. --no-package-lock also leaves the lock alone but ignores it: SDK drifts to 1.32.2, 158 packages, a real cost. For a correct lock, a plain re-install leaves it byte-identical anyway, so --no-save costs nothing there. Recommendation: add --no-save to both install calls in a follow-up, if Getty agrees that the launcher should never write a plugin's tracked files.

